-- ===== POST 1: Ture and the man's fish =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$Ture and the man's fish$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Ture is the trickster spider who appears in almost every traditional Zande folktale. The story below, recorded in Mrs Edward Clive Gore's 1921 collection <em>Sangba Ture</em>, tells of his greed and the price he paid for it.</p><p>One day, a Zande man went to the forest pools to fish. He spent hours baling water from a pool by hand until he had filled a basket with small, sweet fish. As he rested beside his catch, Ture crept through the bushes, eyeing the fish. He wanted them badly, but had no wish to do the work of catching them himself.</p><p>So Ture turned to <em>sanza</em>, the Zande art of speech with a hidden, double meaning. He ran up to the fisherman in apparent panic and cried: "My friend! Look at the sky, your termites are swarming this very moment! Leave your fish with me and go and gather your harvest, or you will lose it."</p><p>Fearing he would miss the termite swarm, the fisherman thanked Ture and hurried into the forest. As soon as his back was turned, Ture grabbed the basket of fish and made off home to feast.</p><p>When the fisherman reached his termite mounds, he found them silent, with no swarming in sight. Realising he had been tricked, he ran back to the pool, but his basket was empty and Ture was gone. "It is witchcraft," he cried, as the Azande do when sudden misfortune strikes.</p><p>To find the culprit, he consulted the termite oracle, a method open to men, women and children alike. He placed a branch of the <em>dakpa</em> tree and a branch of the <em>kpoyo</em> tree into a termite mound and waited overnight for an answer.</p><p>By morning, the termites had eaten only the <em>dakpa</em> branch, a sign that pointed to Ture. The villagers went to his home and found him asleep, his stomach full. The tale is still told to children today, as a reminder that greed brings swift shame, and that the oracles will always uncover those who use <em>sanza</em> to deceive their neighbours.</p>$body$
WHERE id = '0564b691-1b4a-4be4-9f53-32fa5f1f9849';

-- ===== POST 2: Ture, Leopard and the rubbing-board oracle =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$Ture, Leopard and the rubbing-board oracle$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Ture, the trickster spider and central figure of Zande folklore, was walking through the forest one day when he caught the smell of roasting meat. Leopard had just returned from a successful hunt and was preparing a feast. Ture, greedy as ever, decided he would have the meat for himself, and turned to <em>sanza</em>, the Zande art of speech with a hidden, double meaning, to get it.</p><p>He approached Leopard's homestead and said: "The forest path is wide, but sometimes the shadow of a wooden granary turns cold, and a man is struck down at the very moment he seeks its shelter." He hoped the riddle would confuse Leopard long enough for him to slip away with the food.</p><p>Leopard was suspicious. To protect his feast, he consulted <em>benge</em>, the poison oracle used for serious matters. He mixed the alkaloid paste and gave it to a fowl, asking: "If Ture means to trick me and steal my meat, let the fowl die." The fowl went into spasms and died. The oracle's verdict, as always, was final.</p><p>Ture, meanwhile, wanted to know whether his trick would work. He visited his one true friend, the operator of his rubbing-board oracle, and asked: "Will I trick Leopard and eat his meat today?" As the question was put, the wooden handle stuck fast to the board, a clear warning. "If you go back to Leopard," his friend told him, "you will be punished severely."</p><p>Ture ignored the warning. He crept back to Leopard's home that night to steal the meat while Leopard slept, but Leopard was waiting for him. In the struggle that followed, Leopard threw Ture into the cooking fire and burned his back, giving rise to the separate tale "How Ture burnt Leopard all over". Ture fled into the forest, sore and regretting that he had not listened to the oracle.</p><h2>What the story means</h2><p>The tale was first published in Mrs Edward Clive Gore's 1921 collection <em>Sangba Ture</em>. Like many Ture stories, it carried lessons for Zande listeners beyond entertainment.</p><ul><li><strong>True friendship:</strong> the story is the source of a well-known Zande proverb, that Ture had only one operator of the rubbing-board oracle. It means that while a person may know many people, they have only one true friend willing to tell them the truth.</li><li><strong>Moral limits:</strong> by showing Ture ignoring the oracle's warning out of greed, the story taught Zande children the consequences of breaking taboos and disregarding trusted counsel.</li></ul>$body$
WHERE id = 'd9191f40-18a3-4b1d-89a0-4ee3045f35ed';

-- ===== POST 3: What makes the Zande language distinctive =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$What makes the Zande language distinctive$title$,
  body = $body$<p>The Zande language, spoken across South Sudan, the Democratic Republic of Congo and the Central African Republic, has several features that set it apart from its neighbours.</p><h3>1. A language that crosses three borders</h3><p>Zande, known natively as Pazande, is spoken by an estimated 1.8 million people as a first language, making it the largest of the Zande languages. Speakers live across the north-east of the Democratic Republic of Congo, western South Sudan and the eastern Central African Republic.</p><h3>2. Counting on fingers and toes</h3><p>The traditional Zande number system is base 20, built around counting fingers and toes. Because the language historically had no standalone words for numbers above ten or twenty, larger numbers are described rather than named.</p><ul><li>The number 20 is <em>boro ru e</em>, meaning "a person stands it", a reference to a person's 20 fingers and toes.</li><li>Numbers from six to nine use the verb <em>bati</em>, meaning "to step over". Six, for example, is <em>bisue bati sa</em>, literally "five, step over one".</li><li>Larger numbers follow the same logic: 30 is expressed as "20 plus 10", and 120 as <em>kama na ue bawe</em>, "one hundred and two tens".</li></ul><h3>3. A grammar built on rare structures</h3><p>Zande grammar includes several features rarely found in European languages.</p><ul><li><strong>Words that wrap around other words:</strong> some modifiers split in two and surround the word they describe. The demonstrative "this" (<em>gi...re</em>) encloses a noun, so "this man" becomes <em>gi ko re</em>. Negation works the same way, wrapping around a verb phrase with <em>nga...te</em> or <em>nga...ya</em>.</li><li><strong>Separate pronouns for people and animals:</strong> Zande has distinct pronouns and possessives for humans, animals and inanimate objects. Animals take their own forms, such as <em>u</em> and <em>ru</em>, and their own possessives, such as <em>gau</em> ("its", of an animal) and <em>gaami</em> ("theirs", of animals), separate from the inanimate possessive <em>aa</em>.</li><li><strong>A dropped syllable:</strong> nouns ending in <em>-se</em> lose that syllable when a possessive is attached. <em>Ngbaduse</em> ("chest") becomes <em>ngbadure</em> ("my chest").</li><li><strong>Matching adjectives:</strong> adjectives and verb modifiers copy the class of the noun they describe, a feature linguists call pronoun copying.</li><li><strong>Chained verbs:</strong> sequential actions are linked in a single sentence using the marker <em>ki</em>, as in <em>Ko a ndu ki mangi e ki yega</em>, "he went, did it, and came home".</li></ul>$body$
WHERE id = '66240dcf-ae5b-4fb6-9a6d-cf0ef79b5b3d';

-- ===== POST 4: A guide to Zande pronouns =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$A guide to Zande pronouns$title$,
  body = $body$<h2>Subject and object pronouns</h2><p>Zande distinguishes between personal pronouns, for humans and an indefinite form, and a separate set for animals. Inanimate nouns have no distinct personal pronouns of their own.</p><ul><li><strong>I / me:</strong> subject <em>mi</em>, object <em>re</em></li><li><strong>You (singular):</strong> subject <em>mo</em>, object <em>ro</em></li><li><strong>He / him:</strong> subject <em>ko</em>, object <em>ko</em></li><li><strong>She / her:</strong> subject <em>ro</em>, object <em>ri</em></li><li><strong>He or she (indefinite):</strong> subject <em>ni</em>, object <em>ni</em></li><li><strong>We / us:</strong> subject <em>ani</em>, object <em>rani</em></li><li><strong>You (plural):</strong> subject <em>oni</em>, object <em>roni</em></li><li><strong>They / them:</strong> subject <em>i</em> or <em>yo</em>, object <em>yo</em></li><li><strong>It (animal):</strong> subject <em>u</em>, object <em>ru</em></li><li><strong>They / them (animals):</strong> subject <em>ru</em>, object <em>ra</em></li></ul><p>The object forms of animal pronouns, such as <em>ru</em>, are also used as suffixes on nouns to show close possession, such as with body parts. When the noun ends in <em>-se</em>, that syllable is dropped: <em>ngbaduse</em> ("chest") becomes <em>ngbadure</em> ("my chest").</p><h2>Possessive pronouns</h2><p>Zande possessives add a third category, for inanimate objects, alongside the human and animal forms.</p><ul><li><strong>Mine:</strong> <em>gimi</em></li><li><strong>Yours (singular):</strong> <em>gamo</em></li><li><strong>His:</strong> <em>gako</em></li><li><strong>Hers:</strong> <em>gari</em></li><li><strong>His or hers (indefinite):</strong> <em>gani</em></li><li><strong>Its (animal):</strong> <em>gau</em></li><li><strong>Its (inanimate):</strong> <em>gaa</em></li><li><strong>Ours:</strong> <em>gaani</em></li><li><strong>Yours (plural):</strong> <em>gaoni</em></li><li><strong>Theirs:</strong> <em>gayo</em></li><li><strong>Theirs (animals):</strong> <em>gaami</em></li></ul><p>Possessive pronouns can also work reflexively, as in <em>Mi ye ti gimi</em>, "I have come myself".</p><h2>Reflexive pronouns</h2><p>Zande has singular reflexive forms for human, animal and inanimate classes.</p><ul><li><strong>Myself / ourselves:</strong> <em>tire</em> / <em>tirani</em></li><li><strong>Yourself / yourselves:</strong> <em>tiro</em> / <em>tironi</em></li><li><strong>Himself / themselves (masculine):</strong> <em>tiko</em> / <em>tiyo</em> or <em>tira</em></li><li><strong>Herself / themselves (feminine):</strong> <em>tiri</em> / <em>tiyo</em> or <em>tira</em></li><li><strong>Itself (animal):</strong> <em>tiru</em></li><li><strong>Itself (inanimate):</strong> <em>tie</em></li></ul>$body$
WHERE id = '4f2d5602-7306-4d7b-b4a1-bc3a463af920';

-- ===== POST 5: What role does the akpaningba xylophone play in Zande dance? =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$What role does the akpaningba xylophone play in Zande dance?$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Carved from tree trunks and strung with keys of dried banana stem, the akpaningba is the instrument the Zande people of Central Africa build their dances around. Researchers describe it as far more than accompaniment: it is the pulse a whole village moves to, and a way of saying in music what might otherwise go unsaid.</p><p>Also spelled akpaningbo or kpaningbo, the instrument belongs to a family of African log xylophones found across the Congo basin, where related versions go by dozens of local names. Among the Zande, spread today across South Sudan, the Democratic Republic of Congo and the Central African Republic, this design has given its name to an entire genre of circle dance built around it, the kpaningba.</p><h2>An instrument made to be shared</h2><p>The akpaningba is what organologists call a "loose-key" xylophone: individual wooden bars are laid across a resonating base, sometimes bundles of reeds or banana stems, rather than fixed to a frame. Field recordings from Zande territory show it typically built and played by three or four men seated on either side, each responsible for a different part of the melody. One archival description from a Zande village near Juba calls it simply the beating heart of both the music and the village, since it is usually the only strictly melodic instrument in an ensemble otherwise built from drums, bells and rattles.</p><p>Tuning is precise, not approximate. Recordings catalogued by the International Library of African Music show the akpaningba's bars tuned to a fixed sequence of pitches that matches the tuning of Zande harp strings, meaning the instrument shares a tonal vocabulary with the rest of Zande music rather than standing apart from it. Younger players often learn on scaled-down versions: one documented example, made by a boy from three sections of tree trunk with five keys of banana stem, shows how directly the skill passes down within families and villages.</p><h2>At the centre of the circle</h2><p>In performance, the akpaningba does not sit at the edge of a dance. It sits at the centre of one. The kpaningba dance is performed by participants moving in a circle around the instrument and its players, taking small, unison steps that carry the ring of dancers gradually anti-clockwise. A recurring gesture among Zande dancers involves holding the hands out with palms upward, gently raising and lowering the forearms in time with the beat.</p><p><strong>The xylophone does not follow the dancers. The dancers follow the xylophone.</strong></p><p>From that central position, the instrument effectively conducts the event: its players set and shift the tempo, cue changes in the song, and give singers their starting pitch. Because the dancers and singers are, in a real sense, responding to what the akpaningba players do, researchers who have documented the tradition describe the instrument as the anchor around which the whole performance, rhythm, song and choreography together, is organised.</p><h2>More than entertainment</h2><p>Zande communities have long treated music and dance as one of the main channels through which collective feeling is expressed, alongside storytelling and song. Occasions built around the akpaningba have served, at different times, as outlets for straightforward celebration, such as weddings, harvests and homecomings, and as a space in which a community's mood, including frustration or a shared hope for better circumstances, could be voiced through song lyrics carried on the xylophone's rhythm, in a culture where direct complaint was not always the easiest route.</p><p>This dual role echoes something wider in Zande expressive culture. The same community that developed <em>sanza</em>, the art of layered, double-meaning speech used in everyday conversation, also built a musical form in which a joyful surface can carry a more serious undertone. A dance that looks, to an outsider, like pure celebration may also be doing quieter work, airing what a community feels but does not always say outright.</p><h2>Made by the people who play it</h2><p>Unlike instruments bought ready-made, the akpaningba is typically built by the same local artisans, often musicians themselves, who go on to perform on it. That link between maker and player reinforces its status as a genuinely communal object rather than specialised equipment. Locally, it is understood less as "an instrument" in the abstract sense and more as a living expression of Zande identity, craftsmanship and collective memory.</p><h2>A tradition under pressure</h2><p>Decades of displacement in South Sudan, driven by civil conflict, have scattered many Zande communities away from the settled village life in which traditions such as the kpaningba dance were normally passed from elders to children. Cultural bodies, including UNESCO's office in South Sudan, have pointed to the country's traditional dances as part of an intangible heritage under strain, and have backed festivals that bring different ethnic groups together to perform and safeguard their dance traditions as a contribution to peacebuilding and national cohesion.</p><p>At a more local level, Zande village associations and dance groups continue to organise around instruments like the akpaningba specifically to keep the skills of building and playing it alive, teaching the next generation of musicians rather than letting the tradition lapse with an ageing generation of players.</p><p>For now, the akpaningba remains in active use in the villages where it is still built. Whether at a wedding, a harvest gathering or simply an evening when musicians decide to play, the instrument continues to do what it has apparently done for generations: pull a community into a circle, set its feet moving in step, and give shape, in rhythm rather than words, to whatever the moment calls for.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>World Music Network / Riverboat Records, sleeve notes for <em>Wayo: Trance Percussion Masters of South Sudan</em> (2014)</li><li>International Library of African Music (ILAM) field recordings, "Kponingbo xylophone" and "Zande dance", via SAMAP, University of KwaZulu-Natal</li><li>Pitt Rivers Museum, Southern Sudan Project, E. E. Evans-Pritchard photographic collection</li><li>Royal Museum for Central Africa (AfricaMuseum), "Anemba" instrument entry</li><li>UNESCO, "Celebrating culture, identity and diversity for social cohesion, unity and peace in South Sudan"</li><li>Encyclopaedia Britannica, "South Sudan: Music, Dance, Poetry"</li></ul>$body$
WHERE id = '28fa9fa2-fc7f-445c-b46e-bd7d0cdc90e2';
